    "text": "Let us say that a positive integer \\(k\\) has property \\(P\\) if, for every integer base \\(b\\) with\n\\[\n3\\le b<k,\n\\]\nthe base-\\(b\\) expansion of \\(k\\) contains at most two distinct digits. We prove that \\(43\\) has property \\(P\\), and that no integer larger than \\(43\\) has it.\n\nWe use the following elementary observation repeatedly. If \\(b\\ge2\\) and\n\\[\nN=b^2+cb+d,\n\\qquad 0\\le c<b,\\quad 0\\le d<b,\n\\]\nthen the base-\\(b\\) expansion of \\(N\\) is \\((1,c,d)_b\\). Indeed, division by \\(b\\) first gives remainder \\(d\\) and quotient \\(b+c\\), and a second division gives remainder \\(c\\) and quotient \\(1\\). Consequently, if \\(1,c,d\\) are pairwise distinct, then this expansion contains three distinct digits. If in addition \\(3\\le b<N\\), this one base is enough to show that \\(N\\) does not have property \\(P\\).\n\nFirst we check that \\(43\\) does have property \\(P\\). In bases \\(3,4,5,6\\), its expansions are\n\\[\n43=(1121)_3=(223)_4=(133)_5=(111)_6.\n\\]\nThese use respectively the digit sets \\(\\{1,2\\}\\), \\(\\{2,3\\}\\), \\(\\{1,3\\}\\), and \\(\\{1\\}\\). For every \\(b\\) with \\(7\\le b<43\\), we have \\(43<b^2\\), so the base-\\(b\\) expansion of \\(43\\) has at most two digit positions and therefore at most two distinct digits. Thus \\(43\\) has property \\(P\\).\n\nWe next dispose of the finite interval \\(44\\le k\\le288\\). The following table supplies, for every such \\(k\\), a base \\(b\\in\\{3,4,5,6,7,8,9\\}\\) and the base-\\(b\\) expansion of \\(k\\). An entry \\(k:b(w)\\) means that \\(w\\) is the base-\\(b\\) expansion of \\(k\\). Each displayed word contains at least three distinct digit symbols. The expansions are obtained by successive Euclidean divisions by the indicated base; thus every entry exhibits an allowed base \\(3\\le b<k\\) in which \\(k\\) has more than two distinct digits.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{rrrrr}\n44:4(230)&45:3(1200)&46:3(1201)&47:3(1202)&48:3(1210)\\\\\n49:4(301)&50:4(302)&51:3(1220)&52:4(310)&53:5(203)\\\\\n54:4(312)&55:3(2001)&56:4(320)&57:3(2010)&58:3(2011)\\\\\n59:3(2012)&60:6(140)&61:3(2021)&62:6(142)&63:3(2100)\\\\\n64:3(2101)&65:3(2102)&66:3(2110)&67:4(1003)&68:6(152)\\\\\n69:3(2120)&70:4(1012)&71:4(1013)&72:4(1020)&73:3(2201)\\\\\n74:4(1022)&75:3(2210)&76:4(1030)&77:4(1031)&78:4(1032)\\\\\n79:4(1033)&80:5(310)&81:6(213)&82:4(1102)&83:3(10002)\\\\\n84:5(314)&85:5(320)&86:3(10012)&87:3(10020)&88:3(10021)\\\\\n89:3(10022)&90:6(230)&91:4(1123)&92:3(10102)&93:7(162)\\\\\n94:4(1132)&95:3(10112)&96:3(10120)&97:3(10121)&98:3(10122)\\\\\n99:3(10200)&100:3(10201)&101:3(10202)&102:3(10210)&103:3(10211)\\\\\n104:3(10212)&105:3(10220)&106:3(10221)&107:3(10222)&108:4(1230)\\\\\n109:4(1231)&110:3(11002)&111:4(1233)&112:4(1300)&113:3(11012)\\\\\n114:3(11020)&115:3(11021)&116:3(11022)&117:5(432)&118:4(1312)\\\\\n119:3(11102)&120:4(1320)&121:4(1321)&122:4(1322)&123:3(11120)\\\\\n124:4(1330)&125:6(325)&126:3(11200)&127:3(11201)&128:3(11202)\\\\\n129:3(11210)&130:9(154)&131:4(2003)&132:3(11220)&133:4(2011)\\\\\n134:4(2012)&135:3(12000)&136:3(12001)&137:3(12002)&138:3(12010)\\\\\n139:3(12011)&140:3(12012)&141:3(12020)&142:3(12021)&143:3(12022)\\\\\n144:3(12100)&145:3(12101)&146:3(12102)&147:3(12110)&148:4(2110)\\\\\n149:5(1044)&150:3(12120)&151:4(2113)&152:4(2120)&153:3(12200)\\\\\n154:3(12201)&155:3(12202)&156:3(12210)&157:4(2131)&158:4(2132)\\\\\n159:3(12220)&160:5(1120)&161:4(2201)&162:6(430)&163:3(20001)\\\\\n164:4(2210)&165:3(20010)&166:3(20011)&167:3(20012)&168:8(250)\\\\\n169:3(20021)&170:5(1140)&171:3(20100)&172:3(20101)&173:3(20102)\\\\\n174:3(20110)&175:3(20111)&176:3(20112)&177:3(20120)&178:3(20121)\\\\\n179:3(20122)&180:4(2310)&181:3(20201)&182:4(2312)&183:3(20210)\\\\\n184:3(20211)&185:3(20212)&186:6(510)&187:3(20221)&188:4(2330)\\\\\n189:3(21000)&190:3(21001)&191:3(21002)&192:3(21010)&193:3(21011)\\\\\n194:3(21012)&195:3(21020)&196:3(21021)&197:3(21022)&198:3(21100)\\\\\n199:3(21101)&200:3(21102)&201:3(21110)&202:4(3022)&203:4(3023)\\\\\n204:3(21120)&205:4(3031)&206:4(3032)&207:3(21200)&208:3(21201)\\\\\n209:3(21202)&210:3(21210)&211:4(3103)&212:4(3110)&213:3(21220)\\\\\n214:4(3112)&215:5(1330)&216:4(3120)&217:3(22001)&218:4(3122)\\\\\n219:3(22010)&220:3(22011)&221:3(22012)&222:4(3132)&223:3(22021)\\\\\n224:4(3200)&225:3(22100)&226:3(22101)&227:3(22102)&228:3(22110)\\\\\n229:4(3211)&230:4(3212)&231:3(22120)&232:4(3220)&233:4(3221)\\\\\n234:6(1030)&235:3(22201)&236:4(3230)&237:3(22210)&238:5(1423)\\\\\n239:5(1424)&240:5(1430)&241:4(3301)&242:4(3302)&243:5(1433)\\\\\n244:4(3310)&245:3(100002)&246:4(3312)&247:5(1442)&248:3(100012)\\\\\n249:3(100020)&250:3(100021)&251:3(100022)&252:7(510)&253:5(2003)\\\\\n254:3(100102)&255:5(2010)&256:5(2011)&257:3(100112)&258:3(100120)\\\\\n259:3(100121)&260:3(100122)&261:3(100200)&262:3(100201)&263:3(100202)\\\\\n264:3(100210)&265:3(100211)&266:3(100212)&267:3(100220)&268:3(100221)\\\\\n269:3(100222)&270:4(10032)&271:4(10033)&272:3(101002)&273:5(2043)\\\\\n274:4(10102)&275:3(101012)&276:3(101020)&277:3(101021)&278:3(101022)\\\\\n279:4(10113)&280:4(10120)&281:3(101102)&282:4(10122)&283:4(10123)\\\\\n284:3(101112)&285:3(101120)&286:3(101121)&287:3(101122)&288:3(101200).\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nFor completeness, the arithmetic meaning of such an entry is entirely elementary. For example, \\(44=2\\cdot4^2+3\\cdot4+0=(230)_4\\), and \\(245=1\\cdot3^5+0\\cdot3^4+0\\cdot3^3+0\\cdot3^2+0\\cdot3+2=(100002)_3\\). All other entries are read and checked in exactly the same positional way. Hence no \\(k\\) from \\(44\\) through \\(288\\) has property \\(P\\).\n\nIt remains to prove uniformly that every \\(k\\ge289\\) fails. Put\n\\[\nm=\\lfloor\\sqrt{k}\\rfloor,\n\\qquad s=k-m^2.\n\\]\nThe defining inequalities for the integer square root give\n\\[\nm^2\\le k<(m+1)^2=m^2+2m+1.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n0\\le s\\le2m.\n\\]\nAlso \\(k\\ge289=17^2\\), so \\(m\\ge17\\). We divide the possible values of \\(s\\) into ten exhaustive cases. In each case we choose a base among \\(m,m-1,m-2,m-3\\) and display three pairwise distinct digits.\n\n1. Suppose \\(s=0\\). Take \\(b=m-3\\). Since \\(m=b+3\\),\n\\[\nk=m^2=(b+3)^2=b^2+6b+9=(169)_b.\n\\]\nHere \\(b=m-3\\ge14\\), so \\(6\\) and \\(9\\) are valid digits, and \\(1,6,9\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n2. Suppose \\(s=1\\). Take \\(b=m-2\\). Since \\(m=b+2\\),\n\\[\nk=m^2+1=(b+2)^2+1=b^2+4b+5=(145)_b.\n\\]\nNow \\(b\\ge15\\), and \\(1,4,5\\) are pairwise distinct valid digits.\n\n3. Suppose \\(2\\le s\\le m-1\\). Use base \\(b=m\\). Then\n\\[\nk=m^2+s=(10s)_m,\n\\]\nwhere the notation \\((10s)_m\\) means that the three digits, from most to least significant, are \\(1,0,s\\). The bound \\(s<m\\) makes \\(s\\) a valid digit, and \\(s\\ge2\\) makes \\(1,0,s\\) pairwise distinct.\n\n4. Suppose \\(s=m\\). Take \\(b=m-1\\), so \\(m=b+1\\). Then\n\\[\nk=m^2+m=(b+1)^2+(b+1)=b^2+3b+2=(132)_b.\n\\]\nSince \\(b\\ge16\\), these are valid digits, and \\(1,3,2\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n5. Suppose \\(s=m+1\\). Take \\(b=m-2\\), so \\(m=b+2\\) and \\(s=b+3\\). Hence\n\\[\nk=(b+2)^2+(b+3)=b^2+5b+7=(157)_b.\n\\]\nHere \\(b\\ge15\\), and \\(1,5,7\\) are pairwise distinct valid digits.\n\n6. Suppose\n\\[\nm+2\\le s\\le2m-4.\n\\]\nTake \\(b=m-1\\), and define\n\\[\nt=s-(m+2).\n\\]\nThen \\(m=b+1\\), \\(s=b+3+t\\), and \\(t\\ge0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nt\\le(2m-4)-(m+2)=m-6=b-5.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(4\\le t+4\\le b-1\\). We calculate\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nk&=m^2+s\\\\\n &= (b+1)^2+(b+3+t)\\\\\n &=b^2+3b+(t+4).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the base-\\(b\\) digits are \\(1,3,t+4\\). They are valid, and because \\(t+4\\ge4\\), they are pairwise distinct.\n\n7. Suppose \\(s=2m-3\\). Take \\(b=m-1\\), so \\(m=b+1\\). Then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nk&=(b+1)^2+2(b+1)-3\\\\\n &=b^2+4b=(140)_b.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe digits \\(1,4,0\\) are pairwise distinct and valid because \\(b\\ge16\\).\n\n8. Suppose \\(s=2m-2\\). Take \\(b=m-3\\), so \\(m=b+3\\). Then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nk&=(b+3)^2+2(b+3)-2\\\\\n &=b^2+8b+13=(1,8,13)_b.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHere \\(b\\ge14\\), so \\(13<b\\), and \\(1,8,13\\) are pairwise distinct valid digits.\n\n9. Suppose \\(s=2m-1\\). Take \\(b=m-1\\), so \\(m=b+1\\). Then\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nk&=(b+1)^2+2(b+1)-1\\\\\n &=b^2+4b+2=(142)_b.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe digits \\(1,4,2\\) are pairwise distinct and valid.\n\n10. Finally, suppose \\(s=2m\\). Use base \\(b=m\\). Then\n\\[\nk=m^2+2m=(120)_m,\n\\]\nand the digits \\(1,2,0\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nThese ten cases are exhaustive: starting at \\(0\\), they cover\n\\[\n0,\\ 1,\\ [2,m-1],\\ m,\\ m+1,\\ [m+2,2m-4],\\ 2m-3,\\ 2m-2,\\ 2m-1,\\ 2m.\n\\]\nFor \\(m\\ge17\\), the indicated intervals occur in this order and together contain every integer from \\(0\\) through \\(2m\\).\n\nIt remains only to note that every base used above is an allowed base. Each is at least \\(m-3\\ge14\\), hence at least \\(3\\). Also each is at most \\(m\\), while\n\\[\nk\\ge m^2>m\n\\]\nbecause \\(m\\ge17\\). Thus in every case \\(3\\le b<k\\). We have therefore exhibited, for every \\(k\\ge289\\), an allowed base in which \\(k\\) has three distinct digits. Such a \\(k\\) cannot have property \\(P\\).\n\nCombining the finite calculation for \\(44\\le k\\le288\\) with the uniform argument for \\(k\\ge289\\), no integer greater than \\(43\\) has property \\(P\\). Since \\(43\\) itself does have property \\(P\\), the sequence is finite and its last term is \\(43\\).",
